_ratio\" giving conditions \"R160\", \"R100\", \"R50\" per... but config_level values are {condition, value} — the values would need to also vary by model. The config_level shape: {attribute, axis, values: [{condition, value}]}. It doesn't include model dimension. So combining DN×ratio into config_level would lose model dimension.\n\nBetter: make model_id = \"DN25/R160\" etc. Then model_level entries:\n- overload_flowrate_q4 per model\n- permanent_flowrate_q3 per model\n- transitional_flowrate_q2 per model\n- minimum_flowrate_q1 per model\n- nominal_diameter per model\n- q3_q1_ratio per model\n\n30 models × 6 attributes. That's big but accurate. Alternatively use matrix_tables for the four DN-group tables and model_level for main attributes.\n\nGiven the schema emphasizes matrix detection as most important, I'll do:\n\nmodel_family:\n  family_name: \"MAGB1\"\n  models: 30 entries with model_id like \"MAGB1 DN25 R160\"? Or just \"DN25 R160\". I'll use \"DN25 R160\".\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The Q2/Q1 values: For DN25: Q3=16, Q1 = 16/160=0.10, 16/100=0.16, 16/50=0.32. Q2 = 1.6×Q1: 0.16, 0.256→0.26, 0.51 (0.512→0.51). Yes matches table.\n\nDN32: Q3=25: Q1 = 0.16, 0.25, 0.50; Q2 = 0.25(0.256), 0.40, 0.80. Matches.\n\nDN40: Q3=40: Q1 = 0.25, 0.40, 0.80; Q2 = 0.40, 0.64, 1.28. Matches.\n\nDN50: Q3=63: Q1 = 0.40 (63/160=0.394→0.40), 0.63, 1.26; Q2 = 0.63 (0.630→0.63? 1.6×0.394=0.630), 1.01 (1.6×0.63=1.008→1.01), 2.02. Matches.\n\nDN65: Q3=100: Q1=0.63, 1.00, 2.00; Q2=1.00, 1.60, 3.20. Matches.\n\nDN80: Q3=160: Q1=1.00, 1.60, 3.20; Q2=1.60, 2.56, 5.12. Matches.\n\nDN100: Q3=250: Q1=1.56, 2.50, 5.00; Q2=2.50, 4.00, 8.00. Matches.\n\nDN125: Q3=400: Q1=2.50, 4.00, 8.00; Q2=4.00, 6.40, 12.80. Matches.\n\nDN150: Q3=630: Q1=3.94, 6.30, 12.60; Q2=6.30, 10.08, 20.16. Matches.\n\nDN200: Q3=1000: Q1=3.94(1000/160=6.25? wait). Hmm DN200: Q3=1000. Q1 = 1000/160 = 6.25? But table says 3.94, 6.30, 12.60. Hmm, 630/160 = 3.94. That's the DN150 values repeated? DN200 row: Q2 = 6.30, 10.08, 20.16; Q1 = 3.94, 6.30, 12.60. These equal 630-based values (Q3=630 for DN150). But DN200 Q3=1000. Q1 should be 6.25, 10.00, 20.00 and Q2 = 10, 16, 32. The table shows values copied from DN150? Interesting — the OCR shows for DN200-300 table: Q2: 6.30/10.08/20.16 for all three DNs, Q1: 3.94/6.30/12.60 for all three. That looks like possibly an error in the certificate or in OCR, but I must extract as-is (don't fabricate/correct). So DN200, DN250, DN300 all have Q1 = 3.94, 6.30, 12.60 and Q2 = 6.30, 10.08, 20.16 per the table. Hmm wait — but that might genuinely be what the certificate says. I'll extract as given.\n\nWait, DN200 Q4 = 787.5, Q3 = 630? No wait: DN200 Q4 = 787.5, Q3 = 630? Table says: DN200: Q4=787.5, Q3=630; DN250: Q4=1250, Q3=1000; DN300: Q4=2000, Q3=1600. Wait let me re-read:\n\n\"Overload flowrate(Q4)[m³/h]: 787.5 | 1250 | 2000\" — columns DN200, DN250, DN300.\n\"Permanent flowrate(Q3)[m³/h]: 630 | 1000 | 1600\".\n\nHmm wait, DN150 had Q4=788, Q3=630. And DN200 Q4=787.5, Q3=630. OK so DN200 Q3=630, matching Q1=3.94 etc. Actually 630/160 = 3.9375 → 3.94. Yes! So DN200's Q1/Q2 values are consistent with Q3=630. Good, no error. I misread earlier — DN200 Q3 is 630, not 1000. Let me recheck: \"Permanent flowrate(Q3)[m³/h]<td>630</td><td>1000</td><td>1600</td>\" for DN200, DN250, DN300. Yes. So Q1: DN200 (Q3=630): 3.94, 6.30, 12.60. DN250 (Q3=1000): 3.94?? 1000/160 = 6.25, not 3.94. Table says Q1 row: 3.94 | 6.30 | 12.60 | 3.94 | 6.30 | 12.60 | 3.94 | 6.30 | 12.60. So for DN250 and DN300 the same values as DN200. 1000/160 = 6.25 → Q1 would be 6.25; table says 3.94. Hmm, that's inconsistent, but perhaps genuine (certificate may contain such quirk) or OCR duplication. Rule: don't fabricate; extract as-is. I'll extract the values as shown.\n\nHmm, but should I? The instruction: extract as in markdown. Yes, extract as-is.\n\nNow DN150 Q4: 788; DN200 Q4: 787.5. Fine.\n\nIndicating range: DN25-40: 99999; DN50: 99999 (colspan 3 for 99999, colspan 6 for 999999 → DN50: 99999, DN65 & DN80: 999999); DN100-150: 999999; DN200-300: 9999999.\n\nLength L: DN25-80: 200; DN100: 250, DN125: 250, DN150: 300; DN200: 350, DN250: 400, DN300: 500.\n\nSoftware version: DN25-150: \"Version 1.0.0.25\"; DN200-300: \"10.34\" with Checksum 25668. Interesting difference. Firmware version 10.28 for first tables; the label in figure shows FW10.33 — ignore label specifics; label example.\n\nElectromagnetic environment class: E1 (compact), E2 (remote).\n\nBattery: 3.6 V. Min battery life: 5 years. Low flow cut off: 1% of nominal flowrate.\n\nResolution of indicating device: 0.001 m³ (normal), 0.000001 m³ (calibration).\n\nTemperature class T50; water pressure class MAP 10; pressure loss class ΔP10; flow profile sensitivity U5 D3; orientation any; climatic class B; accuracy class 2.\n\nMPE1 = ±5%; MPEu = ±2% (≤30°C), ±3% (>30°C).\n\nNow, type_level characteristics (constant across models):\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- ratio_q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP 10\"\n- pressure_loss_class: \"ΔP 10\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_classes: \"U5 D3\"\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- connection_type: \"flange\"\n- climatic_environment_class: \"B\"\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: varies by version — compact E1, remote E2. That's a config axis! Config: version (compact/remote) → E1/E2. Use config_level.\n- mpe_lower_zone: -5% to +5%? value ±5% → represent as {min: -5, max: 5}, unit \"%\".\n- mpe_upper_zone: two temperature conditions → config_level with axis \"water_temperature\": ≤30°C → ±2%, >30°C → ±3%.\n- resolution_indicating_device: 0.001 normal / 0.000001 calibration → config_level axis \"display_mode\".\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V.\n- minimum_battery_life: 5 years.\n- low_flow_cutoff: 1 % of nominal flowrate → value 1, unit \"%\", or value \"1 % of nominal flowrate\"? Better: value 1, unit \"%\", and maybe note. I'll do value: 1, unit \"%\" — but \"from nominal flowrate\" is reference. Could add reference string. Keep simple: value 1, unit \"%\", maybe include the reference in attribute? Hmm — I could do characteristic low_flow_cutoff with value \"1 % of nominal flowrate\"? Rule says numbers not strings. I'll use value 1, unit \"%\", and add a separate config? No — keep as {value: 1, unit_symbol: \"%\"} and maybe a footnote-like note isn't possible. Alternatively value: {min:1,max:1}? No. Keep 1%.\n\nModel_level attributes per model (30 model variants):\n- nominal_diameter (mm)\n- overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h)\n- permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h)\n- transitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h)\n- minimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h)\n- q3_q1_ratio (dimensionless)\n- indicating_range (m³) — varies\n- length (mm) — varies\n- software_version — varies (string values). Model_level values allow strings? The structured value shape: value can be number, {min,max}, or string. For model_level entries, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. The value inside should follow structured form? The example in rule 4 shows values: [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}] — no unit_symbol per value. Hmm, the example shows plain value. But rule 3 says structured values STRICT for every characteristic value. The R137 example shows \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}] with unit at attribute level (unit_symbol: \"m³/h\" at the attribute level). So for model_level, the unit is at the attribute level, and value is plain. OK, follow that: model_level entries have attribute-level unit_symbol and plain values.\n\nActually looking at the R137 example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, ...]}. Yes, unit at attribute level, values plain numbers. I'll follow that pattern (it's the canonical example in the prompt).\n\nSimilarly config_level example not given in detail, but rule 5: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" So config_level values have unit_symbol per entry.\n\nNow — model_id naming. Options: \"DN25 R160\", \"DN25 R100\", \"DN25 R50\", ..., \"DN300 R50\". 30 models. Labels could include details.\n\nAlternatively, simpler: 10 models (per DN) and treat ratio as config axis. But config_level can't capture model×ratio matrix. Hmm, but I could keep both: model_level for q4/q3/DN/length/indicating_range (per DN models), and config_level for q1/q2 with axis \"q3_q1_ratio\" — but those values also depend on DN. The config_level values {condition, value} would be ambiguous across DNs (condition \"R160\" → which DN's q1?). That loses info.\n\nI'll go with 30 models: model_id like \"DN25 R160\". Each model's attributes: nominal_diameter 25, q3_q1_ratio 160, q4, q3, q2, q1, indicating_range, length, software_version.\n\nThat's 30 models × ~9 attributes = large but correct. Let me consider JSON size — fine.\n\nActually, hmm, maybe I should also include matrix_tables for the four DN-group tables to preserve structure. Rule 4 says extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_family/model_level. For multi-DN tables with Q2/Q1 sub-columns by ratio, the matrix table would have columns: dn, q4, q3, q2_r160, q2_r100, q2_r50, q1_r160, q1_r100, q1_r50, plus type details. I could emit one matrix_table per page table (4 tables: DN25-40, DN50-80, DN100-150, DN200-300). That preserves the Q3/Q1 ratio variant structure.\n\nLet me design matrix_tables:\n\nTable 1 (DN25–40):\ncolumns: [\"nominal_diameter_dn\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"ratio_q3_over_q1\"]\ncolumn_units: [\"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]\nrows: each DN row expanded with 3 ratio variants:\n{\"nominal_diameter_dn\": 25, \"overload_flowrate_q4\": 20, \"permanent_flowrate_q3\": 16, \"transitional_flowrate_q2\": 0.16, \"minimum_flowrate_q1\": 0.10, \"ratio_q3_over_q1\": 160}\n... etc for each ratio.\n\nPlus shared rows in \"description\" or a second set of common characteristics. I could include common attributes in a \"type_details\" description. Hmm, matrix_tables need name, columns, rows, description, footnotes. I'll add description mentioning constant values (Q2/Q1=1.6, Q4/Q3=1.25, accuracy class 2, etc.). Or keep matrix table focused on flow data and put constants in type_level.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: one matrix_table per DN-group capturing the varying columns only, with the shared attributes in description. Let me do 4 matrix tables:\n\n1. \"Basic technical data DN25 to DN40\" — rows for 9 combos (3 DN × 3 ratio).\n2. \"Basic technical data DN50 to DN80\" — 9 combos.\n3. \"Basic technical data DN100 to DN150\" — 9 combos.\n4. \"Basic technical data DN200 to DN300\" — 9 combos.\n\nAnd a separate matrix table? The remaining varying attributes per DN (indicating_range, length, software_version) differ across groups. I could include them as columns too... but they're not per-ratio. Including them repeated per row is fine: indicating_range same for the 3 ratios of a DN. That makes the matrix table richer. Columns: dn, q4, q3, q2, q1, q3_q1_ratio, indicating_range, length, software_version. Hmm, software_version: \"1.0.0.25\" vs \"10.34\" — string values. OK.\n\nWait, also check page 5 second table (DN200–300 continuation): Software version \"10.34\", checksum \"25668\", no firmware version row. First tables: Software version \"Version 1.0.0.25\", Firmware version \"Version 10.28\". So software_version values: \"1.0.0.25\" (DN25–150) and \"10.34\" (DN200–300). firmware_version: \"10.28\" (DN25–150), not specified for DN200–300. Hmm, \"Version 10.28\" — extract as \"10.28\".\n\nChecksum 25668 for DN200–300.\n\nLet me now decide model_id naming: \"MAGB1 DN25 R160\"? or \"DN25/R160\"? I'll use \"DN25 R160\" (clean). Models list 30 entries. Each model could also carry attributes in model entry? Schema: models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I can put the key attributes in attributes{} — e.g. {\"nominal_diameter\": 25, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 160}. That's helpful. But then model_level characteristics also carry the values. Slight duplication but acceptable. The R60 example shows models with only model_id. I'll include model_id and maybe minimal attributes (dn, ratio). To reduce size, maybe just model_id... but attributes{} is in schema and useful. I'll include dn and ratio in attributes; keep it light.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider: is 30 models overkill? The alternative reading: the certificate covers type MAGB1 with DN variants; the \"model\" is MAGB1 with DN as configuration. The R137 example treats size G as models. By analogy, DN is the model axis. The Q3/Q1 ratio variants (R160/R100/R50) are additional variants within each DN — like configuration options. They change Q1/Q2 only. So models = DN sizes (10), and ratio = config axis... but again config_level lacks model dimension.\n\nCompromise: models = 10 DNs. model_level attributes: q4, q3, dn, length, indicating_range, software_version. Then for q1 and q2, emit matrix per model... can't in config_level. Alternatively emit model_level entries with attribute \"minimum_flowrate_q1_r160\" etc.? That's hacky.\n\nI think 30 models with combined ids is cleanest and most information-preserving. model_id: \"DN25 R160\" etc. Let me generate all.\n\nDN25: R160 (q4=20, q3=16, q2=0.16, q1=0.10), R100 (q2=0.26, q1=0.16), R50 (q2=0.51, q1=0.32).\nDN32: q4=31.3, q3=25; R160: q2=0.25, q1=0.16; R100: q2=0.40, q1=0.25; R50: q2=0.80, q1=0.50.\nDN40: q4=50, q3=40; R160: q2=0.40, q1=0.25; R100: q2=0.64, q1=0.40; R50: q2=1.28, q1=0.80.\nDN50: q4=78.8, q3=63; R160: q2=0.63, q1=0.40; R100: q2=1.01, q1=0.63; R50: q2=2.02, q1=1.26.\nDN65: q4=125, q3=100; R160: q2=1.00, q1=0.63; R100: q2=1.60, q1=1.00; R50: q2=3.20, q1=2.00.\nDN80: q4=200, q3=160; R160: q2=1.60, q1=1.00; R100: q2=2.56, q1=1.60; R50: q2=5.12, q1=3.20.\nDN100: q4=312.5, q3=250; R160: q2=2.50, q1=1.56; R100: q2=4.00, q1=2.50; R50: q2=8.00, q1=5.00.\nDN125: q4=500, q3=400; R160: q2=4.00, q1=2.50; R100: q2=6.40, q1=4.00; R50: q2=12.80, q1=8.00.\nDN150: q4=788, q3=630; R160: q2=6.30, q1=3.94; R100: q2=10.08, q1=6.30; R50: q2=20.16, q1=12.60.\nDN200: q4=787.5, q3=630; R160: q2=6.30, q1=3.94; R100: q2=10.08, q1=6.30; R50: q2=20.16, q1=12.60.\nDN250: q4=1250, q3=1000; R160: q2=6.30, q1=3.94; R100: q2=10.08, q1=6.30; R50: q2=20.16, q1=12.60.\nDN300: q4=2000, q3=1600; R160: q2=6.30, q1=3.94; R100: q2=10.08, q1=6.30; R50: q2=20.16, q1=12.60.\n\nWait — DN250 Q3=1000 but Q1=3.94? 1000/160=6.25. The table as OCR'd shows 3.94/6.30/12.60 repeated for all three. Hmm. Let me double check the markdown for the DN200-300 table:\n\n\"Transitional flowrate(Q2)[m³/h]<td>6.30</td><td>10.08</td><td>20.16</td><td>6.30</td><td>10.08</td><td>20.16</td><td>6.30</td><td>10.08</td><td>20.16</td>\"\n\"Minimum flowrate(Q1)[m³/h]<td>3.94</td><td>6.30</td><td>12.60</td><td>3.94</td><td>6.30</td><td>12.60</td><td>3.94</td><td>6.30</td><td>12.60</td>\"\n\nYes, repeated. And Q3: 630, 1000, 1600. So per the document, DN250 (Q3=1000) has Q1 = 3.94 for R160 — inconsistent with Q3/Q1=160, but I extract as-is. Actually wait, maybe DN250's Q3=1000 and Q1=3.94 doesn't match; the real certificate probably had different values but our job is extraction, not correction. Extract as shown.\n\nIndicating range:\n- Group 1 (DN25-40): 99999 (all).\n- Group 2 (DN50-80): DN50 → 99999; DN65, DN80 → 999999.\n- Group 3 (DN100-150): 999999 (all).\n- Group 4 (DN200-300): 9999999 (all).\n\nLength:\n- Group 1&2: 200 all.\n- Group 3: DN100: 250, DN125: 250, DN150: 300.\n- Group 4: DN200: 350, DN250: 400, DN300: 500.\n\nSoftware version: groups 1-3: \"1.0.0.25\"; group 4: \"10.34\" (with checksum 25668).\nFirmware version: groups 1-3: \"10.28\"; group 4: not listed (omit). Actually the group 4 table (page 5 continuation) doesn't have firmware version. The label example shows FW10.33 but that's a specific label; ignore.\n\nNow build JSON.\n\ncertificate:\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-CZ-16.03\",\n  \"scheme\": \"B\",\n  \"revision\": \"1\"? — schema doesn't have revision in certificate, but revision_history exists. The certificate says \"Revision 1\". Is there revision history with changes? No changes described. Hmm. revision_history entries: {revision, date, changes}. We know revision 1 exists but no changes/date per revision. The date_issued 28 June 2017. Should I add revision_history entry {revision: \"1\", date: \"2017-06-28\", changes: null}? That's reasonable — the certificate is Revision 1 issued 2017-06-28. Changes unknown → omit changes? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll include {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2017-06-28\"} without changes. Hmm, changes is part of entry shape; omitting is fine.\n\n  \"project_number\": omit,\n  \"page_total\": 10,\n  \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2017-06-28\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": omit\n}\n\nissuing_authority:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Czech Metrology Institute\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Okružní 31,\", \"638 00 Brno, CZ\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"Jan Kalandra\",\n  ...\n}\n\nHmm, and Pavel Klenovsky signed as \"The OIML Issuing Authority\". Could put person_title? No — person_title belongs with person_responsible. Maybe add a second field? Not in schema. I could note in person_title: null. I'll leave title out. Alternatively person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and add \"person_title\": null — omit.\n\nWait — maybe better to reflect: The signature block \"The OIML Issuing Authority / Pavel Klenovsky / 28 June 2017\". Since schema has no signer field, I might put person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" (explicit label). Good.\n\napplicants: [{name: \"Arkon Flow Systems, s.r.o.\", address_lines: [\"Berkova 534/92, 612 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same.\n\ncertified_type:\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"MAGB1\"],\n  \"module_designation\": null,\n  \"description\": \"The water meter type MAGB1 is electromagnetic water meter. There are two modifications: compact and remote version. ...\" — long. I'll include a condensed version? Rule says free-text description if present. Include the full English description? It's long but fine. I'll include it, maybe trimmed slightly but faithful. I'll include the full paragraph text (combining). Actually include as-is (the English text). I'll paste the description paragraphs.\n}\n\nmodel_family:\n{\n  \"family_name\": \"MAGB1\",\n  \"models\": [30 entries]\n}\n\nEach model: {\"model_id\": \"DN25 R160\", \"label\": \"MAGB1 DN25, Q3/Q1 = R160\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter_dn\": 25, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 160}}. That's verbose ×30. Maybe simpler: {\"model_id\": \"DN25 R160\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter_dn\": 25, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 160}}. Drop label to reduce size. I'll include model_id and attributes.\n\ncomponents: The cert mentions compact/remote versions — versions, not named components. Also \"electronic calculating/indicating device\", frequency output, RS485 — optional equipment. Components not named with type designations. I could record a component for the \"electronic calculating/indicating device\"? Rule 6: include only if cert names sub-assemblies. It mentions \"electronic calculating/indicating device\" generically and optional \"frequency output\" and \"RS 485\". Hmm. These are optional interfaces, not type-designated components. I'll skip components to avoid noise. Or include? The description says meters \"equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device\" and \"can be equipped by frequency output ... or RS 485\". I think it's borderline; I'll skip components (no type designations given). Actually — maybe include with alternatives OR: frequency output OR RS485? They're optional accessories. The schema's components are meant for named sub-assemblies. I'll omit.\n\ncharacteristics:\n\ntype_level:\n- accuracy_class: {value: 2, unit_symbol: null, unit_id: null}\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: {value: 1.6}\n- ratio_q4_over_q3: {value: 1.25}\n- temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_pressure_class: {value: \"MAP 10\"} — hmm \"MAP 10\" means max admissible pressure 10 bar? Keep as string \"MAP 10\". Or max_admissible_pressure: 10, unit bar? Not stated as bar in doc. Keep \"MAP 10\".\n- pressure_loss_class: {value: \"ΔP 10\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_classes: {value: \"U5 D3\"}\n- orientation_limitation: {value: \"any\"}\n- connection_type: {value: \"flange\"}\n- climatic_environment_class: {value: \"B\"}\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- minimum_battery_life: {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"years\"}\n- low_flow_cutoff: {value: 1, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — hmm the unit \"% from nominal flowrate\". unit_symbol \"%\": value 1. Add \"low_flow_cutoff_reference\"? Not needed; attribute name suffices: low_flow_cutoff_percent_of_nominal_flowrate? I'll name attribute \"low_flow_cutoff\" with value 1, unit \"%\" — the reference \"from nominal flowrate\" lost. Alternative: keep unit_symbol null and value \"1 % of nominal flowrate\"? Rule prefers numeric. I'll use attribute \"low_flow_cutoff\" {value: 1, unit_symbol: \"%\"} and maybe that's acceptable. Hmm, or make it clearer: \"low_flow_cutoff_relative_to_nominal_flowrate\": {value: 1, unit_symbol: \"%\"}. I'll use that name.\n- indicating_range: varies → model_level.\n- resolution: config_level.\n- MPE: mpe_lower_zone → type_level {value: {min:-5,max:5}, unit_symbol: \"%\"}. mpe_upper_zone → config_level axis \"water_temperature\": conditions \"≤ 30°C\" → ±2%, \"> 30°C\" → ±3%.\n- electromagnetic_environment_class → config_level axis \"version\": compact → E1, remote → E2.\n- max cable length remote version 6 m: from description. Could add type_level? It's for remote version → config_level axis version: remote → 6 m; compact → 0/none. I'll add config_level: attribute \"max_cable_length\", axis \"version\", values: [{condition: \"remote\", value: 6, unit_symbol: \"m\"}]. Compact not specified. OK.\n- RS485 max cable length 3 m: attribute \"rs485_max_cable_length\" type_level {value: 3, unit_symbol: \"m\"}.\n- straight inlet/outlet length: inlet 5×DN, outlet 3×DN. Could record as strings: \"inlet_straight_length\": \"5 × DN\"? Value string formula allowed. Add: inlet_straight_pipe_length: {value: \"5 × DN\"}? Hmm \"5 times the diameter\". I'll add inlet_straight_length: {value: \"5 DN\", ...}? Might be noisy. Description covers it. Skip or include as string values. I'll include in description only.\n- display digits: up to 9 digits. type_level: display_digits: {value: 9}? \"can show up to 9 digits\" → {value: {min: null, max: 9}}? Simpler: max_display_digits: {value: 9}. Include.\n- software versions per model group → model_level (string values).\n\nmodel_level entries (attribute, unit_symbol, values[]):\n1. nominal_diameter_dn, \"mm\", 30 values (or per DN 10 values — with 30 models each DN repeated 3×; that's redundant but consistent). Hmm, redundancy: DN same across the 3 ratio variants. It's fine.\n\nActually, wait. Maybe I should reconsider: keep 10 models per DN, and encode ratio variants via model_id suffix anyway... no. Let me just go with 30 models and repeat DN/q4/q3 across ratio variants; q1/q2/ration differ. Consistent model_level entries:\n\n- nominal_diameter_dn (mm): 30 values (each DN ×3).\n- overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h): 30 values.\n- permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h): 30 values.\n- q3_q1_ratio: 30 values (160/100/50).\n- transitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h): 30 values.\n- minimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h): 30 values.\n- indicating_range (m³): 30 values (99999/999999/9999999).\n- body_length (mm): 30 values (200/250/300/350/400/500).\n- software_version: 30 values (\"1.0.0.25\" / \"10.34\").\n\nThat's 9 × 30 = 270 value entries. Long but thorough. Alternatively use matrix_tables for the 4 tables with all columns and skip some model_level? Rule 4 says both. The R137 example populated model_level with only the key varying attributes (qmax, qt, qmin) — not every column (it skipped cyclic_volume, max_pressure_loss, pmax in model_level, keeping them in matrix table only). So I can populate model_level with the core flow attributes and keep the rest in matrix_tables. \n\nPlan:\n- model_level: q4, q3, q2, q1, q3_q1_ratio, nominal_diameter. (6 attributes × 30)\n- matrix_tables: 4 tables capturing full rows including indicating_range, length, software_version (and noting constants in description).\n\nHmm, but then indicating_range/length/software_version aren't in model_level — acceptable per example pattern. Actually, let me also add length and indicating_range and software_version to model_level for completeness? The example kept pmax etc. only in matrix table. I'll follow the example: core attributes in model_level, everything in matrix_tables. But indicating_range varies across models and is a metrologically important attribute... I'll include indicating_range and length and software_version in model_level too. It's more complete; JSON is long but fine.\n\nActually, let me reduce: 30 models each repeated 3× for DN/q4/q3 feels wasteful but it's the accurate encoding of the variant space. Fine.\n\nLet me now also double check Q4 values: DN25: 20; DN32: 31.3; DN40: 50; DN50: 78.8; DN65: 125; DN80: 200; DN100: 312.5; DN125: 500; DN150: 788; DN200: 787.5; DN250: 1250; DN300: 2000.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. {attribute: \"electromagnetic_environment_class\", axis: \"meter_version\", values: [{condition: \"compact\", value: \"E1\"}, {condition: \"remote\", value: \"E2\"}]}\n2. {attribute: \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_zone\", axis: \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"≤ 30°C\", value: ...}]} — value ±2% → {min:-2,max:2}? The structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}: value can be object {min: -2, max: 2}, unit_symbol \"%\". So:\n   {\"condition\": \"water temperature ≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"water temperature > 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n3. {attribute: \"indicating_device_resolution\", axis: \"display_mode\", values: [{condition: \"normal\", value: 0.001, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}, {condition: \"calibration\", value: 0.000001, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}]}\n   Note: description says high resolution 0.001 L = 0.000001 m³. Consistent.\n4. {attribute: \"max_cable_length\", axis: \"meter_version\", values: [{condition: \"remote\", value: 6, unit_symbol: \"m\"}]}\n\ntype_level as listed above.\n\nmatrix_tables (4):\nEach: name, columns, column_units, rows, description.\n\nColumns: [\"nominal_diameter_dn\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", \"indicating_range\", \"length\", \"software_version\"]\nunits: [\"mm\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",null,\"m³\",\"mm\",null]\n\nRows (9 per table, DN×ratio):\n\nTable 1 DN25–40:\nDN25 R160: dn25, q4 20, q3 16, q2 0.16, q1 0.10, ratio 160, indicating 99999, length 200, sw \"1.0.0.25\"\nDN25 R100: 0.26, 0.16\nDN25 R50: 0.51, 0.32\nDN32: q4 31.3, q3 25; R160 0.25/0.16; R100 0.40/0.25; R50 0.80/0.50\nDN40: q4 50, q3 40; R160 0.40/0.25; R100 0.64/0.40; R50 1.28/0.80\n\nTable 2 DN50–80:\nDN50: q4 78.8, q3 63; R160 0.63/0.40; R100 1.01/0.63; R50 2.02/1.26; indicating 99999\nDN65: q4 125, q3 100; R160 1.00/0.63; R100 1.60/1.00; R50 3.20/2.00; indicating 999999\nDN80: q4 200, q3 160; R160 1.60/1.00; R100 2.56/1.60; R50 5.12/3.20; indicating 999999\nlength 200 all.\n\nTable 3 DN100–150:\nDN100: q4 312.5, q3 250; R160 2.50/1.56; R100 4.00/2.50; R50 8.00/5.00; indicating 999999; length 250\nDN125: q4 500, q3 400; R160 4.00/2.50; R100 6.40/4.00; R50 12.80/8.00; length 250\nDN150: q4 788, q3 630; R160 6.30/3.94; R100 10.08/6.30; R50 20.16/12.60; length 300\n\nTable 4 DN200–300:\nDN200: q4 787.5, q3 630; R160 6.30/3.94; R100 10.08/6.30; R50 20.16/12.60; indicating 9999999; length 350; sw \"10.34\" (checksum 25668)\nDN250: q4 1250, q3 1000; same q2/q1; length 400\nDN300: q4 2000, q3 1600; same; length 500\n\nDescription fields: mention constants (ratio Q2/Q1 1.6, Q4/Q3 1.25, class 2, T50, MAP10, ΔP10, U5D3, B, E1/E2, flange, battery 3.6V/5y, low flow cutoff 1%). Maybe only in one place — I could add a fifth \"common characteristics\" note in each table description briefly. Or add a type_details table? I'll put a brief description in each matrix table.\n\nrecommendation:\n{\n  \"id\": \"R49\",\n  \"edition\": 2013,\n  \"scheme\": ... hmm — scheme in recommendation? The schema shows scheme under recommendation too (e.g. MAA?). It says recommendation: id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes. The certificate: \"R 49, edition 2013, for accuracy class 2\". scheme — the cert scheme B; include \"B\"? The recommendation's scheme field likely refers to e.g. \"MPE+/MPE−...\" no. I'll include scheme: \"B\"? Hmm, risky. Actually for consistency with certificate scheme, maybe omit scheme in recommendation or set \"B\". I'll set \"B\". Or omit. I'll omit to be safe? The schema lists it; but the doc doesn't attribute scheme to recommendation. The certificate itself is basic → scheme B. I'll put scheme: \"B\" in both places? Hmm. Let me think: in previous examples of this schema family, recommendation.scheme isn't typical. I'll omit scheme from recommendation, keep accuracy_classes: [2]. Hmm, actually accuracy class is \"2\" — as number or string? Class 2 — I'll use 2 (number) or \"2\"? Classes sometimes \"Y\" etc. I'll use \"2\" string? Numbers feel fine: [2]. Hmm the value structured rule applies to characteristics; recommendation accuracy_classes is a list. I'll use [2] as numbers. Or strings \"2\". I'll go with numbers.\n}\n\ntest_reports[]:\nFrom page 2: six test reports:\n1. No. 6015-PT-P3014-16, from 26 May 2016, 184 pages, role: type_evaluation_report (the \"associated OIML Basic Type Evaluation Report\"?). Hmm — the first is the main type evaluation report; others are additional test reports. Roles: \"type_evaluation_report\" for 6015-PT-P3014-16; \"test_report\" for the others.\n2. 8551-PT-E0096-16, 23 May 2016, 26 pages.\n3. 8551-PT-E0097-16, 23 May 2016, 35 pages.\n4. 6015-PT-P3037-17, 20 June 2017, 23 pages.\n5. 8553-PT-S1004-17, 31 January 2017, 5 pages.\n6. 8553-PT-S1012-17, 6 June 2017, 6 pages.\n\nDates: 2016-05-26, 2016-05-23, 2016-05-23, 2017-06-20, 2017-01-31, 2017-06-06.\n\nrevision_history: [{\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2017-06-28\"}]. Hmm, is revision 1 issued 28 June 2017? The cert says Revision 1 and date 28 June 2017. Reasonable.\n\nfootnotes: none present.\n\nDescription: I'll include the \"Measuring system description\" text. Let me write it (English):\n\n\"The water meter type MAGB1 is electromagnetic water meter. There are two modifications: compact and remote version. The water meters type MAGB1 are intended for metering cold potable water and hot water, based on an inductive principle, PTFE and hard rubber lining, with straight inlet (5 times the diameter) and outlet (3 times the diameter) length, without flow conditioner and there are equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device. The maximum cable length for remote version is 6 meters. The display shows the measurements in cubic meter volume (positive, negative, total and auxiliary) and cubic meter per hour flow rate. The meter is not designed to measure reverse flow. The meter does not require any extra-mechanical housing or adjustments. The meter is intended for mount to the connecting any pipework with the flow axis in the horizontal and vertical (from bottom to top and from top to bottom) plane and with the indicating device positioned at the top and at the side. The meter is equipped with the electronic indicating device. The display is a digital type, and can show up to 9 digits. The normal resolution mode is used during normal operation. The water meter displays in the normal resolution mode up to 000000.001 m³/h flow rate and 000.001 m³ volume on the digital display. The water meter displays the volume resolution of 0.001 L on the digital display in the high resolution mode which would be used during the calibration process. This mode is set up by factory tool (software had to be attached). The water meters type MAGB1 can be equipped by frequency output which can be used for remote reading or by RS 485 (with maximum cable length 3 m).\"\n\nOK, include full.\n\nNow — type_level keys with unit fields. Format per rule 2: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"unit_id\": ..., \"footnote_markers\": [...]}. For entries without footnotes, footnote_markers: [] or omit? I'll omit empty ones (rule 8: omit if not present; but footnote_markers empty is fine to omit). I'll include footnote_markers only when non-empty.\n\nunit_id: leave null unless certain. I'll just omit unit_id or set null? Schema shows unit_id in shape. Example sets null. To keep JSON smaller I'll include unit_id: null only... hmm \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain\". I'll include \"unit_id\": null for numeric values with units. For strings like \"T50\", unit_symbol null. Should I include unit_symbol: null explicitly? The R137 example at model level omitted unit for ratio. For type_level, I'll include unit_symbol only when there is one, and unit_id: null when there's a unit I can't map... Actually to be safe and consistent, for each structured value object I'll include \"value\", \"unit_symbol\" (or omit if none), \"unit_id\": null when unit_symbol present. Let me include both keys where relevant.\n\nLet me now write out model_level arrays.\n\nModels (30): \nDN25 R160, DN25 R100, DN25 R50\nDN32 R160, DN32 R100, DN32 R50\nDN40 R160, DN40 R100, DN40 R50\nDN50 R160, DN50 R100, DN50 R50\nDN65 R160, DN65 R100, DN65 R50\nDN80 R160, DN80 R100, DN80 R50\nDN100 R160, DN100 R100, DN100 R50\nDN125 R160, DN125 R100, DN125 R50\nDN150 R160, DN150 R100, DN150 R50\nDN200 R160, DN200 R100, DN200 R50\nDN250 R160, DN250 R100, DN250 R50\nDN300 R160, DN300 R100, DN300 R50\n\nWait, that's 12 DNs × 3 = 36 models! I listed 12 DNs: 25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300. Yes