n\nsoftware_version: \"1.0.0.25\" ×27, \"10.34\" ×9.\n\nOK. This is going to be a large JSON. Let me be careful and systematic.\n\nModel IDs order: I'll iterate DN ascending, ratio 160, 100, 50.\n\nFull model list:\n1 DN25 R160\n2 DN25 R100\n3 DN25 R50\n4 DN32 R160\n5 DN32 R100\n6 DN32 R50\n7 DN40 R160\n8 DN40 R100\n9 DN40 R50\n10 DN50 R160\n11 DN50 R100\n12 DN50 R50\n13 DN65 R160\n14 DN65 R100\n15 DN65 R50\n16 DN80 R160\n17 DN80 R100\n18 DN80 R50\n19 DN100 R160\n20 DN100 R100\n21 DN100 R50\n22 DN125 R160\n23 DN125 R100\n24 DN125 R50\n25 DN150 R160\n26 DN150 R100\n27 DN150 R50\n28 DN200 R160\n29 DN200 R100\n30 DN200 R50\n31 DN250 R160\n32 DN250 R100\n33 DN250 R50\n34 DN300 R160\n35 DN300 R100\n36 DN300 R50\n\nmodel_family.models: I'll write each as {\"model_id\": \"DN25 R160\", \"attributes\": {\"nominal_diameter_dn\": 25, \"q3_q1_ratio\": 160}}.\n\nActually, to trim, I could skip attributes in models since model_level carries them. The schema: models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}} — attributes optional. I'll include just model_id to keep size manageable. Hmm, but attributes{} adds machine-readable clarity... model_level already has it. Just model_id.\n\nNow model_level entries:\n\n1) nominal_diameter_dn, unit mm:\n[25,25,25,32,32,32,40,40,40,50,50,50,65,65,65,80,80,80,100,100,100,125,125,125,150,150,150,200,200,200,250,250,250,300,300,300] mapped to models.\n\n2) overload_flowrate_q4, m³/h:\n[20,20,20,31.3,31.3,31.3,50,50,50,78.8,78.8,78.8,125,125,125,200,200,200,312.5,312.5,312.5,500,500,500,788,788,788,787.5,787.5,787.5,1250,1250,1250,2000,2000,2000]\n\n3) permanent_flowrate_q3:\n[16×3,25×3,40×3,63×3,100×3,160×3,250×3,400×3,630×3,630×3,1000×3,1600×3]\n\n4) q3_q1_ratio (no unit):\n[160,100,50] ×12\n\n5) transitional_flowrate_q2:\nDN25: 0.16,0.26,0.51\nDN32: 0.25,0.4,0.8\nDN40: 0.4,0.64,1.28\nDN50: 0.63,1.01,2.02\nDN65: 1.0,1.6,3.2\nDN80: 1.6,2.56,5.12\nDN100: 2.5,4.0,8.0\nDN125: 4.0,6.4,12.8\nDN150: 6.3,10.08,20.16\nDN200: 6.3,10.08,20.16\nDN250: 6.3,10.08,20.