\n  \"issuing_authority\": {...},\n  \"applicants\": [...],\n  \"manufacturers\": [...],\n  \"certified_type\": {...},\n  \"model_family\": {...},\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n    \"config_level\": [...]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"recommendation\": {...},\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...]\n}\n\ntype_level values as objects with value/unit_symbol/unit_id. For strings (T50 etc.): {\"value\": \"T50\", \"unit_symbol\": null}. Include unit_id null? Rule 3 shape includes unit_id. For string values, unit_symbol null and unit_id null. I'll include both keys null for consistency? That adds bulk. The shape says every value MUST be object with that shape — includes unit_symbol and unit_id (footnote_markers optional?). I'll include \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null on string values too, to conform. Footnote_markers: omit when none (or empty). I'll omit when empty.\n\nHmm, \"unit_id\": null everywhere — I'll include it only where unit_symbol is non-null, to reduce noise... Strictness: shape shows unit_id. I'll include unit_id: null alongside unit_symbol: null too. Let me just be consistent: every value object has value, unit_symbol, unit_id (null unless certain), and footnote_markers only if present.\n\nCanonical unit ids I'm fairly certain of in unitsdb... e.g. \"u:degree_Celsius\". For m³/h — unitsdb IDs uncertain. I'll leave all unit_id null per rule (\"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain\").\n\nunit_symbol strings: \"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³\", \"%\", \"V\", \"years\", \"m\".\n\nNow compose description. Full text as extracted. Also I might trim the weird spacing; keep as-is basically.\n\nLet me now also double-check the \"scheme\" letter. OIML-CS: certificates issued under OIML-CS are \"OIML certificates\" — there's also MAA. The prompt schema: scheme (A|B). Hmm — in some OIML contexts: Category A = MAA certificates? Actually OIML-CS defines \"OIML Certificate\" and there are two systems: basic system (no letter) and MAA. Some extraction pipelines use scheme \"A\" for... I recall OIML CS has \"OIML-metrological evaluation (module A)\"? Hmm. Given \"BASIC CERTIFICATE\", I'll use \"B\". I think the intended mapping in this task family is A = MAA, B = basic. Go with \"B\".\n\nRecommendation scheme: \"B\" too? I'll include scheme: \"B\". Hmm, actually let me leave recommendation.scheme out — safer, since the doc attaches scheme to the certificate, not the recommendation. But schema includes it... \"Don't fabricate\" — scheme B is inferred from \"BASIC\". It's a judgment; I'll include scheme \"B\" only at certificate level, omit at recommendation level. Hmm, but then why does schema list scheme under recommendation? Perhaps for MAA-certs where recommendation carries the MAA arrangement. I'll omit there. Decision made.\n\naccuracy_classes: [2] — as number. Or \"2\"? I'll use [2].\n\nEdition: 2013 → int 2013.\n\ndate_issued: \"2017-06-28\".\n\ntest_reports dates:\n- 6015-PT-P3014-16: 2016-05-26, pages 184, role type_evaluation_report.\n- 8551-PT-E0096-16: 2016-05-23, pages 26, role test_report.\n- 8551-PT-E0097-16: 2016-05-23, pages 35, role test_report.\n- 6015-PT-P3037-17: 2017-06-20, pages 23, role test_report.\n- 8553-PT-S1004-17: 2017-01-31, pages 5, role test_report.\n- 8553-PT-S1012-17: 2017-06-06, pages 6, role test_report.\n\nrevision_history: [{\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2017-06-28\"}].\n\nNow config_level detail:\n\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"electromagnetic_environment_class\",\n    \"axis\": \"meter_version\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"compact version\", \"value\": \"E1\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"remote version\", \"value\": \"E2\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_zone\",\n    \"axis\": \"water_temperature\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"water temperature ≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"water temperature > 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"unit_id\": null}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"indicating_device_resolution\",\n    \"axis\": \"display_mode\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"normal mode\", \"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      {\"condition\": \"calibration mode\", \"value\": 0.000001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\", \"unit_id\": null}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"remote_version_max_cable_length\",\n    \"axis\": \"meter_version\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"remote version\", \"value\": 6, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\", \"unit_id\": null}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n\ntype_level:\n\n{\n  \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"ratio_q2_over_q1\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"ratio_q4_over_q3\": {\"value\": 1.25, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_zone\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -5, \"max\": 5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\", ...},\n  \"maximum_admissible_water_pressure_class\": {\"value\": \"MAP 10\"}, — name: water_pressure_class\n  \"pressure_loss_class\": {\"value\": \"ΔP 10\"},\n  \"flow_profile_sensitivity_classes\": {\"value\": \"U5 D3\"},\n  \"orientation_limitation\": {\"value\": \"any\"},\n  \"connection_type\": {\"value\": \"flange\"},\n  \"climatic_environment_class\": {\"value\": \"B\"},\n  \"battery_voltage\": {\"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n  \"minimum_battery_life\": {\"value\": 5, \"unit_symbol\": \"years\"},\n  \"low_flow_cutoff\": {\"value\": 1, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"} — I'll name \"low_flow_cutoff\" and note in... hmm. Let me name it \"low_flow_cutoff\" value 1 unit \"%\" — the \"from nominal flowrate\" part is reference. I could instead do \"low_flow_cutoff\": {\"value\": \"1 % of nominal flowrate\"}? violates numeric preference. Compromise: keep numeric with unit \"%\" and accept slight info loss; description? Not in description. Hmm. I'll add \"low_flow_cutoff_reference\": {\"value\": \"nominal flowrate\"}? That's like the safe_overload_reference example! Rule 3's example: 150% of Emax → separate characteristic safe_overload_reference: \"Emax\". So: \"low_flow_cutoff\": {value 1, \"%\"} + \"low_flow_cutoff_reference\": {value: \"nominal flowrate\"}. Nice, follows the pattern.\n  \"display_max_digits\": {\"value\": 9},\n  \"rs485_max_cable_length\": {\"value\": 3, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"}\n}\n\nAlso maybe \"measurement_principle\": \"electromagnetic\"? In description. Skip type_level. \"medium\": cold and hot potable water — description covers.\n\nModel-level arrays: write now.\n\nI'll write values arrays with model names. Let me produce each:\n\nnominal_diameter — skipping (in model_id). Actually, hmm, decide finally: include or not? Example skipped size. Skip.\n\noverload_flowrate_q4 values:\n[{\"model\":\"DN25 R160\",\"value\":20},{\"model\":\"DN25 R100\",\"value\":20},{\"model\":\"DN25 R50\",\"value\":20},\n {\"model\":\"DN32 R160\",\"value\":31.3},{\"model\":\"DN32 R100\",\"value\":31.3},{\"model\":\"DN32 R50\",\"value\":31.3},\n {\"model\":\"DN40 R160\",\"value\":50},{\"model\":\"DN40 R100\",\"value\":50},{\"model\":\"DN40 R50\",\"value\":50},\n {\"model\":\"DN50 R160\",\"value\":78.8},{\"model\":\"DN50 R100\",\"value\":78.8},{\"model\":\"DN50 R50\",\"value\":78.8},\n {\"model\":\"DN65 R160\",\"value\":125},{\"model\":\"DN65 R100\",\"value\":125},{\"model\":\"DN65 R50\",\"value\":125},\n {\"model\":\"DN80 R160\",\"value\":200},{\"model\":\"DN80 R100\",\"value\":200},{\"model\":\"DN80 R50\",\"value\":200},\n {\"model\":\"DN100 R160\",\"value\":312.5},{\"model\":\"DN100 R100\",\"value\":312.5},{\"model\":\"DN100 R50\",\"value\":312.5},\n {\"model\":\"DN125 R160\",\"value\":500},{\"model\":\"DN125 R100\",\"value\":500},{\"model\":\"DN125 R50\",\"value\":500},\n {\"model\":\"DN150 R160\",\"value\":788},{\"model\":\"DN150 R100\",\"value\":788},{\"model\":\"DN150 R50\",\"value\":788},\n {\"model\":\"DN200 R160\",\"value\":787.5},{\"model\":\"DN200 R100\",\"value\":787.5},{\"model\":\"DN200 R50\",\"value\":787.5},\n {\"model\":\"DN250 R160\",\"value\":1250},{\"model\":\"DN250 R100\",\"value\":1250},{\"model\":\"DN250 R50\",\"value\":1250},\n {\"model\":\"DN300 R160\",\"value\":2000},{\"model\":\"DN300 R100\",\"value\":2000},{\"model\":\"DN300 R50\",\"value\":2000}]\n\npermanent_flowrate_q3: 16,16,16,25,25,25,40,40,40,63,63,63,100,100,100,160,160,160,250,250,250,400,400,400,630,630,630,630,630,630,1000,1000,1000,1600,1600,1600\n\nq3_q1_ratio: 160,100,50 repeated 12 times.\n\nq2: 0.16,0.26,0.51, 0.25,0.4,0.8, 0.4,0.64,1.28, 0.63,1.01,2.02, 1,1.6,3.2, 1.6,2.56,5.12, 2.5,4,8, 4,6.4,12.8, 6.3,10.08,20.16, 6.3,10.08,20.16, 6.3,10.08,20.16, 6.3,10.08,20.16\n\nq1: 0.1,0.16,0.32, 0.16,0.25,0.5, 0.25,0.4,0.8, 0.4,0.63,1.26, 0.63,1,2, 1,1.6,3.2, 1.56,2.5,5, 2.5,4,8, 3.94,6.3,12.6, 3.94,6.3,12.6, 3.94,6.3,12.6, 3.94,6.3,12.6\n\nindicating_range: 99999×12, 999999×15, 9999999×9.\nModels 1–12 → 99999 (DN25,32,40,50). Models 13–27 → 999999 (DN65,80,100,125,150). Models 28–36 → 9999999.\n\nbody_length: models 1–18 → 200; 19–24 → 250; 25–27 → 300; 28–30 → 350; 31–33 → 400; 34–36 → 500.\n\nsoftware_version: models 1–27 → \"1.0.0.25\"; 28–36 → \"10.34\".\n\nmatrix_tables: 4 tables as planned.\n\nNow assemble final JSON. Watch JSON validity: no trailing commas, escape nothing weird. Use \"ΔP 10\" (Unicode fine), \"≤\", \"³\".\n\nFor column_units with nulls: include.\n\nmatrix_tables name: \"Basic technical data DN25–DN40\", etc.\n\nDescriptions for tables:\nt1: \"Basic technical data of water meters type MAGB1, DN25 to DN40. Constant type details: ratio Q2/Q1 = 1.6; ratio Q4/Q3 = 1.25; accuracy class 2; MPE lower zone ±5%; MPE upper zone ±2% (water ≤ 30 °C), ±3% (water > 30 °C); temperature class T50; water pressure class MAP 10; pressure-loss class ΔP 10; resolution 0.001 m³ (normal) / 0.000001 m³ (calibration); flow profile sensitivity classes U5 D3; orientation any; connection flange; climatic class B; electromagnetic class E1 (compact) / E2 (remote); firmware version 10.28; battery 3.6 V; minimum battery life 5 years; low flow cut off 1 % of nominal flowrate.\"\n\nt2: similar, \"DN50 to DN80. Indicating range: 99999 m³ for DN50, 999999 m³ for DN65 and DN80.\"\n\nt3: \"DN100 to DN150...\" same constants.\n\nt4: \"DN200 to DN300. Software version 10.34, checksum 25668. Constant: ... pressure-loss class ΔP 10; indicating range 9 999 999 m³; resolution...; U5 D3; any; flange; B; E1/E2; battery 3.6 V; 5 years; low flow cut off 1 %.\"\n\nI'll write descriptions concisely.\n\ncertified_type: \n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"MAGB1\"],\n  \"module_designation\": null,\n  \"description\": \"...\"\n}\n\nDescription text — include the measuring system description. Let me write it in one string with sentences. That's fine.\n\nShould type_designations include \"MAGB1 Sensor\" (from label figure)? The label example shows \"MAGB1 Sensor\" — it's an example label. I'll keep [\"MAGB1\"].\n\nissuing_authority: include \"person_title\"? Not given for Jan Kalandra. Omit. Include website null? omit.\n\nLet me also double check certificate page_total: \"Page 1 of 10 pages\" → 10.\n\nAlright, also \"date_issued\" — the signature date 28 June 2017 → \"2017-06-28\".\n\nOne more consideration: rule 10 multilingual — no French here. Rule 11: ignore image markers — done.\n\nWrite the final JSON now. Keep it structured and valid. I'll use consistent 2-space-ish formatting mentally; final output just needs to be valid JSON.\n\nAlso decide: include \"footnote_markers\": [] or omit — omit.\n\nFor string-valued structured objects, include unit_symbol: null and unit_id: null. For numeric with units: unit_id: null.\n\nFinal check on model_level attribute naming:\n- \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"indicating_range\", \"body_length\", \"software_version\".\n\nUnits: m³/h ×4, null ratio, m³ indicating, mm length, null software.\n\nOK, writing out. This will be long; be careful with commas and brackets.\n\nLet me draft the models array compactly (36 entries):\n[\"DN25 R160\",\"DN25 R100\",\"DN25 R50\",\"DN32 R160\",\"DN32 R100\",\"DN32 R50\",\"DN40 R160\",\"DN40 R100\",\"DN40 R50\",\"DN50 R160\",\"DN50 R100\",\"DN50 R50\",\"DN65 R160\",\"DN65 R100\",\"DN65 R50\",\"DN80 R160\",\"DN80 R100\",\"DN80 R50\",\"DN100 R160\",\"DN100 R100\",\"DN100 R50\",\"DN125 R160\",\"DN125 R100\",\"DN125 R50\",\"DN150 R160\",\"DN150 R100\",\"DN150 R50\",\"DN200 R160\",\"DN200 R100\",\"DN200 R50\",\"DN250 R160\",\"DN250 R100\",\"DN250 R50\",\"DN300 R160\",\"DN300 R100\",\"DN300 R50\"]\n\nEach as {\"model_id\": \"...\"}.\n\nNow generate all model_level value arrays fully. I'll write them out carefully in the answer.\n\nAlso consider: should indicating_range 99999 be number 99999 — yes. 9999999 number.\n\nLet me write the final answer JSON.",
